        <m>\left(8x+9\right)\cdot 4-4x\cdot 8</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(8x+9\right)^{2}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The function <m>\displaystyle y=\frac{4x}{8x+9}</m> is a quotient of two functions, say <m>f(x)= 4x</m> and <m>g(x)=8x+9</m>.  Then taking the derivatives we get <m>f'(x)=4</m> and <m>g'(x)=8</m>.  Finally, the quotient rule says that 
<me>\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}</me>
so <me>y'= \frac{(8x+9) \cdot (4)  - (4x)\cdot (8)}{(8x+9)^2}= \frac{36}{(8 x + 9)^2}.</me></p>
</solution>
